WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.425
llama.cpp is a low-level local inference ecosystem that supports compatible model formats

00:00:05.425 --> 00:00:06.920
and multiple hardware backends.

00:00:06.920 --> 00:00:10.705
It offers detailed control and can serve as a foundation for command-line tools,

00:00:10.705 --> 00:00:12.343
applications, and local servers.

00:00:12.343 --> 00:00:16.160
Ollama emphasizes straightforward model management and a local service interface.

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:19.945
LM Studio provides a desktop experience for discovering, loading, testing,

00:00:19.945 --> 00:00:21.560
and serving supported models.

00:00:21.560 --> 00:00:25.577
Both can simplify common workflows while retaining runtime-specific limits.

00:00:25.577 --> 00:00:29.620
Open WebUI provides a browser-based interface that can connect to supported local or

00:00:29.620 --> 00:00:30.950
remote model services.

00:00:30.950 --> 00:00:35.040
Text-generation-webui offers a broader experimental interface at a high level.

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:38.785
More features also create more dependencies, settings, and security surface.

00:00:38.785 --> 00:00:42.305
Local APIs allow scripts and applications to call a model service.

00:00:42.305 --> 00:00:46.245
Compatibility with a familiar API shape can ease integration, but authentication,

00:00:46.245 --> 00:00:48.560
network binding, request limits, logging,

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:51.655
and data exposure still require deliberate configuration.

00:00:51.655 --> 00:00:55.540
Model management includes source verification, license review, naming, storage,

00:00:55.540 --> 00:00:59.165
checksums where appropriate, prompt templates, version records, evaluation,

00:00:59.165 --> 00:01:00.080
and retirement.

00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:03.845
A model file is a software and supply-chain dependency, not just content.

00:01:03.845 --> 00:01:06.040
Start with the simplest tool that meets the requirement.

00:01:06.040 --> 00:01:09.955
Validate one model and workflow before adding orchestration, agents, retrieval,

00:01:09.955 --> 00:01:12.740
multiple users, routing, or distributed services.

00:01:12.740 --> 00:01:14.400
Complexity should follow measured need.
